{"track":"precalc","num":"3.04","slug":"double-angle-formulas-and-trigonometric-equations","title":"Double-Angle Formulas and Trigonometric Equations","sections":[{"heading":"Section 1: Double-Angle Formulas","questions":[{"q":"State the double-angle formula for $\\sin(2\\theta)$.","choices":["$2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$","$\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta$","$2\\sin\\theta$","$\\sin^2\\theta$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\sin(2\\theta) = \\sin(\\theta + \\theta) = \\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta + \\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$."},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an equivalent form of $\\cos(2\\theta)$?","choices":["$1 - 2\\cos^2\\theta$","$\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta$","$2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$","$1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta$"],"correct":0,"answer":"The correct forms are $\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta$, $2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$, and $1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta$. $1 - 2\\cos^2\\theta = -\\cos(2\\theta)$."},{"q":"If $\\cos\\theta = 4/5$ with $\\theta$ in Quadrant IV, find $\\sin(2\\theta)$.","choices":["$-24/25$","$24/25$","$-7/25$","$7/25$"],"correct":0,"answer":"In QIV, $\\sin\\theta = -3/5$. $\\sin(2\\theta) = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = 2(-3/5)(4/5) = -24/25$."}]},{"heading":"Section 2: Half-Angle and Power-Reducing Formulas","questions":[{"q":"State the power-reducing formula for $\\sin^2 x$.","choices":["$\\frac{1 - \\cos(2x)}{2}$","$\\frac{1 + \\cos(2x)}{2}$","$\\frac{1 - \\sin(2x)}{2}$","$1 - \\cos^2 x$"],"correct":0,"answer":"From $\\cos(2x) = 1 - 2\\sin^2 x$, solving for $\\sin^2 x$ gives $\\sin^2 x = \\frac{1 - \\cos(2x)}{2}$."},{"q":"Find the exact value of $\\cos(22.5^\\circ)$ using the half-angle formula $\\cos(\\theta/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 + \\cos\\theta}{2}}$.","choices":["$\\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{2} + 1}{2}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 1}{2}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\cos(45^\\circ/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{2}/2}{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}$."},{"q":"Rewrite $\\cos^4 x$ in terms of first powers of cosines of multiple angles.","choices":["$\\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos(2x) + \\frac{1}{8}\\cos(4x)$","$\\frac{1}{4}(1 + \\cos(2x))^2$","$\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos(4x)$","$\\frac{3}{8} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos(2x)$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\left(\\frac{1 + \\cos 2x}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1 + 2\\cos 2x + \\cos^2 2x}{4} = \\frac{1 + 2\\cos 2x + (1 + \\cos 4x)/2}{4} = \\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos(2x) + \\frac{1}{8}\\cos(4x)$."}]},{"heading":"Section 3: Solving Linear Trigonometric Equations","questions":[{"q":"Solve $2\\sin x - 1 = 0$ on the interval $[0, 2\\pi)$.","choices":["$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{11\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi}{6}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\sin x = 1/2$. On $[0, 2\\pi)$, sine is positive in Quadrants I and II at $x = \\pi/6$ and $5\\pi/6$."},{"q":"Solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan x + 1 = 0$ on $[0, 2\\pi)$.","choices":["$\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{11\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi}{6}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\tan x = -1/\\sqrt{3}$. Tangent is negative in Quadrants II and IV at $x = 5\\pi/6$ and $11\\pi/6$."},{"q":"Find all solutions to $\\cos(2x) = 0$ on $[0, \\pi)$.","choices":["$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ only","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$2x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{k\\pi}{2}$. On $[0, \\pi)$, solutions are $\\pi/4$ and $3\\pi/4$."}]},{"heading":"Section 4: Quadratic-Type and Multiple-Angle Equations","questions":[{"q":"Solve $2\\sin^2 x - \\sin x - 1 = 0$ on $[0, 2\\pi)$.","choices":["$\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Factor: $(2\\sin x + 1)(\\sin x - 1) = 0$. $\\sin x = 1 \\Rightarrow x = \\pi/2$. $\\sin x = -1/2 \\Rightarrow x = 7\\pi/6, 11\\pi/6$."},{"q":"Solve $\\sin(2x) = \\cos x$ on $[0, 2\\pi)$.","choices":["$\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$2\\sin x\\cos x - \\cos x = 0 \\Rightarrow \\cos x(2\\sin x - 1) = 0$. $\\cos x = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\pi/2, 3\\pi/2$. $\\sin x = 1/2 \\Rightarrow x = \\pi/6, 5\\pi/6$."},{"q":"Solve $\\cos(2x) + \\cos x = 0$ on $[0, 2\\pi)$.","choices":["$\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\pi, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\pi, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$2\\cos^2 x - 1 + \\cos x = 0 \\Rightarrow (2\\cos x - 1)(\\cos x + 1) = 0$. $\\cos x = 1/2 \\Rightarrow x = \\pi/3, 5\\pi/3$. $\\cos x = -1 \\Rightarrow x = \\pi$."}]}]}